      </Container>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Menu calendar"
          caption="Meals missing a required component get a warning."
          src={rd('provider-menu-calendar')}
          alt="The menu calendar for November 2026 in month view: My meals, Menu calendar, and Shopping list tabs, Today and month controls, a legend for Breakfast, Snack, Lunch, Dinner, and Missing a required food component, and a calendar grid where each day lists meals such as Toast and Bananas, Apples and Cheese ×2, and Pasta and Meatballs, with warning triangles on some breakfasts and today’s date highlighted."
        />
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-system" title="One set of rules for every screen">
    <CaseFor project="logatot" personas={["renee","tanya","marisol"]} stage="audit" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        To keep the screens consistent, I started with the shared pieces: one 154-icon set, a
        contrast-checked pastel palette, and shared helpers for buttons, forms, surfaces, tables,
        and filters. Then I
        rebuilt screens, most-used first.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards label="Design decisions" items={redesignDecisions} />

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <Heading level={3} visual="h2">Accessibility</Heading>
      <p>
        Everything you can say can also be entered by hand. Focus outlines are visible, status
        changes are announced to screen readers, and reports print in black on white.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>


    <Fragment slot="media">
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-8 gap-y-14 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <Plate
            size="half"
            title="Children"
            caption="The roster raised, quick add recessed beside it."
            src={rd('provider-children')}
            alt="The Children page: Active, Inactive, All, and Archived segments, a search field, and a two-column grid of child cards with photos, Active status, and labeled chips such as Diapers, Meds, and Diet. On the right, a recessed Add a child panel with first name, last name, and date of birth fields, an Add child button, and a Use the full form link."
          />
          <Plate
            size="half"
            title="Expenses"
            caption="Totals and filters recessed, the table raised."
            src={rd('provider-expenses')}
            alt="The Expenses page: All expenses, Expense locations, and Expense categories segments, a recessed strip with This month $142.37, This year $600.40, and Receipts 8, a Find expenses panel with a search field and date chips, then a table of expenses with checkboxes, dates, places such as Costco and Target, category badges, amounts, mileage, and edit and delete buttons."
          />
        </div>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">Dark mode</Heading>
        <p>
          Providers log naps in darkened rooms. Dark mode follows the device until
          someone flips the sidebar switch, and every token is contrast-checked in
          both themes. Recessed panels get darker in dark mode, so they stay distinct
          from raised cards.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Voiceboard, dark"
          caption="Module hues keep their meaning on navy."
          src={rd('provider-voiceboard-dark')}
          alt="The Voiceboard for October 30 in dark mode: a near-black sidebar with the Dark mode switch turned on, a navy Ready to listen panel, dark count pills, and a grid of dark child tiles with photos, pickup times, and colored moment chips."
        />
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">Mobile</Heading>
        <p>
          Every rebuilt screen was checked at 390 pixels wide, in both themes and in
          Spanish. Layouts are rearranged for small screens. Calendars become lists,
          tables become cards, and the sidebar becomes a drawer.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <ul class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-5 gap-y-10 md:grid-cols-4">
          {phones.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <Plate size="half" title={p.title} caption={p.note} src={rd(p.slug)} alt={p.alt} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">Public site</Heading>
        <p>
          The homepage previews are the app’s own components with demo data, so the
          site can’t fall out of date. The hero plays a voice demo that respects
          reduced motion and is announced to screen readers.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <ScrollShot
          src="/images/projects/logatot/public-home.webp"
          alt="The full Logatot homepage: a header with an EN/ES switch, the hero “Child Care’s Only Voice-Controlled Assistant” beside a live listening panel and Ava Johnson’s card of the day’s moments, Try it phrase chips, a Why Logatot section with four benefits, a Speak, record, comply section with live Voiceboard tiles, a Ready for anything section with Olivia Bennett’s profile, guardians, and pickups, a Simplified, effective management section with report cards, Pricing at $30 a month or $300 a year, testimonials, and a footer with a contact form."
          label="Logatot homepage"
          caption="Every preview is a live app component with demo data."
        />
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

